C++ Project CI/CD flow and tools

* **CI** = **Continuous Integration**
* **CD** = **Continuous Delivery** or **Continuous Deployment**

In practice:

**CI:** Automatically build and test code whenever developers push changes.

**CD:** Automatically deliver/deploy the tested application to staging or production.

----------------------------------------------
Developer
   │
   │ git push
   ▼
Git Repository
(GitHub / GitLab / Bitbucket)
   │
   ▼
CI Pipeline
   │
   ├── 1. Checkout source
   ├── 2. Install dependencies
   ├── 3. Configure CMake
   ├── 4. Compile
   ├── 5. Run unit tests
   ├── 6. Static analysis
   ├── 7. Code coverage
   ├── 8. Package
   │
   ▼
Artifact
(.deb / .rpm / Docker image / binary)
   │
   ▼
CD Pipeline
   │
   ├── Deploy to Dev
   ├── Integration tests
   ├── Deploy to Staging
   ├── Approval
   └── Deploy to Production

-------------------------

CI/CD tools are language-independent; 
they build and test your C++ project using CMake, GCC/Clang, etc.

| Tool               | CI | CD | Recommendation                  |
| ------------------ | -- | -- | ------------------------------- |
| **GitHub Actions** | ✅  | ✅  | ⭐ Best for learning / GitHub    |
| **GitLab CI/CD**   | ✅  | ✅  | ⭐ Excellent for companies       |
| **Jenkins**        | ✅  | ✅  | Common in enterprise            |
| **Azure DevOps**   | ✅  | ✅  | Good for Microsoft environments |
| **CircleCI**       | ✅  | ✅  | Good alternative                |
-----------------------------------------
If your C++ code is stored in Bitbucket and Jenkins is responsible for CI/CD, 
the flow typically looks like this:

Developer
   │
   │ git push
   ▼
Bitbucket
   │
   │ Webhook
   ▼
Jenkins
   │
   ├── CI
   │    ├── Checkout C++ code
   │    ├── Install dependencies
   │    ├── CMake configure
   │    ├── Compile
   │    ├── Unit tests
   │    ├── clang-tidy
   │    └── Static analysis
   │
   ├── Build Release
   │
   ├── Create Docker image
   │
   └── CD
        ├── Push image to registry
        ├── Deploy Dev
        ├── Integration tests
        ├── Deploy Staging
        └── Deploy Production
----------------------------------------------------
The important distinction

              CI                         CD
              │                          │
Bitbucket → Jenkins → Build → Test → Package → Deploy
                       └───────┬───────┘
                               │
                         CI successful
                               │
                               ▼
                         CD starts

CI = "Is my C++ code buildable and working?"
CD = "Take that tested version and put it into the environment."
